Link Health watches every physical port on the firewall and says which
cable or transceiver is going bad, naming the port the way the chassis
names it (PortA3, SFP+1) instead of the way the driver does.

Every minute it samples the hardware error counters and judges the change
over the window, never the total since boot, so a port that is corrupting
frames now is flagged while a port that logged errors months ago is not.
It also reports a link that negotiated below the fastest speed its own
PHY advertises, which is the usual sign of a broken pair, and reads
transceiver identity and diagnostics on the drivers that expose them.

Each port gets one of seven states on its own page and in a dashboard
widget, and a fault is mailed once, with a confirmation window, a
cooldown and a recovery message. A port carrying too little traffic to
judge is marked as such rather than called healthy, and can be given
traffic to be judged on with the on-demand load test.

